WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.559 --> 00:00:12.139
The humble disc record is not often properly
recognized for the impact its development

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:13.440
had on the modern world.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.660
On the face of it, these records are just
consumer products that allow music to be sold

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.060
to the masses, a concept that itself only
reached the mass market about 100 years ago.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.220
The truth is the impact of this technology
goes way beyond consumption of pop music.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.859
In some way, the record’s closest comparison
is the printing press.

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:36.390
The printing press is often considered one
of the most important inventions in history

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:41.180
due to its ability to quickly and accurately
reproduce ideas in written word; which in

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.379
turn, greatly accelerated the transfer and
exchange of knowledge.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:50.269
The ability to press audio to record so that
it can be reproduced in scale did the exact

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:51.760
same thing for audio.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.939
It functioned as a vector for cultural exchange
and the revolution in recording technology

00:00:56.939 --> 00:01:02.500
ushered in by electronic and audio engineers
that developed the technology is the foundation

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.590
upon which modern communication systems stand
upon.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.420
Of course, these days most of the music we
consume is digital.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.880
The internet has changed not only the way
we consume music, but also the amount of music

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.080
[1] and the music itself [2].

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.140
Curiously while this is happening vinyl is
seeing a year on year increase in sales [3,4].

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.460
Even more curious is the fact that half of
the people who buy an album on vinyl stream

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.369
it first [4] It remains a point of contention
whether analog formats, such as vinyl, are

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:33.909
actually superior to their modern day digital
counterparts.

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:38.100
Is vinyl’s resurgence just consumers seeing
yesteryear’s technology through rose tinted

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.039
glasses, a yearning for a physical connection
to their music or does the music really sound

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:44.600
better on vinyl?

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.939
To understand the differences between these
two we need to first understand the commonalities.

00:01:48.939 --> 00:01:54.820
No matter which format is used, analog or
digital, both require audio data to be created

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.619
by a recording device.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:00.689
The simplest of these would be the microphone
which turns air pressure (sound) into a either

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:06.680
a digital or analog signal, which can then
be replayed as an electrical analog signal.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.260
This process was first put into use in 1877
by Thomas Edison.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.690
While working on the telephone, Edison decided
that it may also be worth investigating if

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.400
sound could be recorded for later reproduction.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.790
Edison designed a rotatable cylinder disc
wrapped in thin foil which was turned by a

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.340
hand crank.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.250
Attached to the disc was a needle, which in
turn was attached to a mouthpiece which adjusted

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.470
the pressure of needle on the disc.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:34.599
Edison talked into mouthpiece while turning
the hand crank at a constant rate and as predicted,

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:39.099
the pressure of the soundwaves imprinted a
proportional indentation which was analogous

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:41.280
to the sound his voice created.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.620
When he finished recording, he returned the
needle to the start.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.599
The indentations which were caused by his
voice could now be played back by rotating

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:49.890
the cylinder.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:56.879
Playback of audio while showing phonograph
working….

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:01.450
This invention, the phonograph, was the first
example of playable recorded sound and for

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:07.769
all intents and purposes the vinyl record
is essentially an iteration of this technology.

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:13.159
The first vinyl recorded was pressed in 1948
by Columbia - specifically, it was this recording

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:14.159
on 12 inch:

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:15.159
- Record plays -
Mendelssohn's Concerto in E Minor by Nathan

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:16.159
Milstein on the violin with the New York Philharmonic

00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:17.769
Vinyl records work on the same basic principle
as Edison’s phonograph.

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:23.120
A 3d representation of a soundwave is physically
pressed on to a vinyl record.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.620
An impression is first created by a cutting
head.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.110
The cutting head creates an impression that
is a direct analog of the soundwave.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:35.200
This process creates a master that will go
to create a stamper that moulds each record.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.329
When a record is played the frequency of the
wave that you will hear will depend on how

00:03:39.329 --> 00:03:44.609
stretched out the wave is on the media and
the volume will depend on the size or amplitude

00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:46.120
of the wave.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.239
This audio information will be pressed on
to vinyl in one of 3 fashions:

00:03:50.239 --> 00:03:53.010
via horizontal modulation
Vertical modulation

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:58.709
OR via a compromise modulation of 45 degrees
Horizontal modulation is always preferable

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:00.530
over vertical modulation.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:05.040
This is because vertical modulation leads
to more distortion and allows for less amplitude

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.849
due to an inability for the stylus to track
the groove and also a propensity for the needle

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:13.349
to bound off the wave if the amplitude is
too high.

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:18.609
But if we run with only horizontal modulation
we can only play audio in mono and we don’t

00:04:18.609 --> 00:04:20.600
have stereo separation of sound.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.520
Accordingly, we use a compromise modulation
of 45 degree in order to allow for separation

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.890
of audio from mono to stereo.

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:32.090
As the stylus follows the groove, it moves
a magnet wrapped in a small coil of copper

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:37.180
wire, this causes an electric current that
corresponds to the groove on the vinyl, which

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.240
in turn corresponds to the physical sound
waves that were originally recorded.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:45.630
The electric current can now cause a physical
movement of the speakers which will reproduce

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.590
that sound pretty faithfully.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:52.470
Some vinyl enthusiasts argue that this smooth
continuous reproduction of sound from analog

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:57.930
to analog is more faithful than digital music
Part of this argument stems from the difference

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:04.090
in how digital music is reproduced - high
quality digital audio data is typically sampled

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.979
44,100 times per second and this data is recorded
in binary format.

00:05:06.979 --> 00:05:11.560
Close inspection of the wave function produced
from binary code shows that rather than the

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:17.050
audio data being smooth and constant like
real life, the audio data is jagged and technically

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.750
non-continuous.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:23.590
Because there is an infinite amount of data
between each second of audio, we have to sample

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:28.090
the audio in regular intervals to minimise
the size of our digital file.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:32.000
Comparing this to the smooth continuous waveform
that is imprinted in vinyl you would think

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.680
this might cause some loss in information.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:40.069
Whether there is loss of information or not
depends on whether the 44,000 sample rate

00:05:40.069 --> 00:05:42.509
is high enough to be functionally the same.

00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:44.910
An answer to this was proposed in 1928 in
a

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:50.240
pivotal paper published by Swedish American
electronic engineer Harry Nyquist [5], and

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.680
was subsequently proven by Claude Shannon
in 1949 [6].

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.770
They simply found that to recreate a frequency
we only need to sample each individual wave

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.229
at least twice.

00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:03.919
If not, the frequency will be digitized with
a lower frequency.

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:09.759
The maximum perceivable frequency a human
ear can detect is 20,000 Hz, and so digital

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:14.890
recordings with a sampling rate of 44,000
Hz can capture even the highest frequency

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:19.949
possible, thus the sound produced by a speaker
using digital audio is effectively the same

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:22.259
sound as analog recordings.

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:26.190
In this case, the argument that analog recordings
are more “faithful” does not meet the

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:31.379
scrutiny of science and in theory digital
and analog music recordings should sound functionally

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:33.940
the same if played on the same equipment.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:38.360
The argument does not end here though, there
are some constraints to how sound can be recorded

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:39.460
on vinyl.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:44.099
Interestingly, these constraints largely explain
both vinyl enthusiasts preference for the

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:49.060
media and also why some might argue that digital
recordings are a superior format for storing

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.240
audio.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.650
The major constraint that impacts vinyl is
simply its limit in data storage.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:59.520
This is simple to understand - A 12 inch record
can only hold so much information in the format

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.930
we’ve described.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.169
Each rotation of the record takes 1.8 seconds.

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:07.580
The next question is how many times each 12
inch can record rotate.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:12.040
Two things affect this, the frequencies found
in bass notes require the groove of the record

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.940
to swing out wider, just as the speaker also
thumbs out wider when it plays base.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:21.500
Waves of higher amplitude that produce louder
sound also require wider grooves.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:25.949
This means that both low frequency sounds
and loud sounds both eat up valuable vinyl

00:07:25.949 --> 00:07:27.240
real estate.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.400
This in turn means that if you’re record
has bass or is loud, like most contemporary

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:35.820
music, there’s not going to be a whole lot
of space on the record for your songs.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:40.389
The net impact of this is that there is a
volume and time constraint on vinyl record

00:07:40.389 --> 00:07:45.000
that does not apply to digital music, which
has huge ramifications for how we listen to

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.370
music, and how music is created and mastered.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:50.770
Vinyl’s limitations do not end here.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:54.970
If the frequency is low and the amplitude
too high (loud), the stylus can become prone

00:07:54.970 --> 00:08:00.199
to bounding off of the wave due to path the
stylus has to take up the wave at speed.

00:08:00.199 --> 00:08:03.919
This can cause the record to bounce around
and skip if not accounted for.

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:08.509
Accordingly, bass needs to be center panned
in the mix and a specific mix has to applied

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:11.840
to music recorded to vinyl to stop this from
happening.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:17.129
High frequency sounds also need to be taking
into consideration while cutting a vinyl record.

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:20.191
The issue is commonly referred to as the “Sibilance
Issue”.

00:08:20.191 --> 00:08:25.080
Sibilance is that unpleasant hissing sound
associated with s sounds and other high frequencies,

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.569
that anyone who has watched my older videos
will be painfully aware of.

00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:32.180
High frequency waves cause two fundamental
problems in vinyl.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:36.380
High frequency sounds mean the waves are very
tight together, the stylus has to surf these

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.849
waves and turn extremely tight corners.

00:08:38.849 --> 00:08:43.620
When the curvature of the groove becomes tighter
than the tip radius of the stylus, the stylus

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:47.240
will begin to plow through the groove and
you will end up with distortion.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.720
On top of this, extremely high frequency waves
can lead to the cutting head that cuts the

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.279
record to overheat.

00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:56.870
This is simply a matter of the cutting head
having to take a longer path and having to

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:58.800
do more work to cut these waves.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.410
The overheating can lead to inaccuracy in
the cutting process and in turn to noise and

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.470
distortion on the final record.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:10.280
To counteract the negative effects that extreme
low end and high end frequencies have on vinyl,

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:14.649
a group of American engineers developed what
became to be known as the Recording Industry

00:09:14.649 --> 00:09:18.350
Association of America (RIAA) curve in the
40s and 50s [7].

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:23.410
The RIAA is a equalization scheme that is
applied to the sound before the master lacquer

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:24.410
is cut.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:28.640
In essence, this curve reduces bass content
and boosts treble in the record.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:33.870
Without this curve, low frequencies take up
so much space that each 12 inch LP would only

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:35.980
allow for 5 minutes of music.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:40.450
In addition, boosting the treble hugely lowers
the surface noise that vinyl can produce due

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:42.579
to the path the stylus takes.

00:09:42.579 --> 00:09:47.140
This is also why a turntable requires a special
phono preamp – in addition to amplifying

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:51.370
the tiny voltage created by the turntable’s
cartridge, the preamp applies the inverse

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:56.850
of the RIAA curve, perfectly restoring the
music’s natural balance and minimizing the

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:00.779
size constraints that are intrinsically linked
to the nature of the media.

00:10:00.779 --> 00:10:04.779
So we’ve painted a complicated and grim
picture for vinyl as a storage media.

00:10:04.779 --> 00:10:09.410
The actual truth here is that there is no
functional difference in audio quality between

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:14.660
digital and analog formats...and studies show
that the human ear and brain is not sufficiently

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:19.310
equipped to distinguish the difference between
sound produced from analog signals when compared

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.150
to a digital counterpart [8].

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:25.130
At the very least, this is enough to debunk
the notion that digital music formats are

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.230
a lesser quality format than analog formats.

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:32.520
An important question to ask here is why are
people, that understand these concepts, still

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:33.520
drawn to vinyl?

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.200
There’s a number of simple answers to this
question:

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.100
Part of it is the nostalgia factor - people
have positive personal associations with the

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:45.029
vinyl format from their youth and these associations
invoke an emotional state that induces a sense

00:10:45.029 --> 00:10:48.959
of comfort [9]; and although there are no
discernible differences in theoretical audio

00:10:48.959 --> 00:10:54.389
quality, vinyl does have a specific sound
that is imparted due to the mastering process.

00:10:54.389 --> 00:10:58.190
Mastering is the process by which the final
song is mixed for the final device it will

00:10:58.190 --> 00:10:59.630
be stored on.

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:03.899
Over the past 36 years, due to the removal
of the physical limitations of vinyl media

00:11:03.899 --> 00:11:08.290
and the spread of digitized music, songs have
become increasingly louder and increasingly

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:09.630
more compressed [10].

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:13.649
In essence, this means that the sound wave
becomes compressed, forcing the quieter parts

00:11:13.649 --> 00:11:18.269
of a song to become relatively louder and
the louder parts relatively quieter, the net

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:20.860
effect being a louder, noisier song.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:25.060
As a result of this trend a vast majority
of commercial music releases have been subject

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:29.350
to a somewhat arbitrary loudness war that
has forced them to increase loudness to keep

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:30.350
pace.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:35.120
It has also resulted in increased use of compression
of the music which some would argue has result

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:38.380
in a loss of detail and nuance in the final
sound.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:42.149
This development has been criticized by a
number of prominent audio engineers [11] and

00:11:42.149 --> 00:11:44.560
is part of the attraction towards vinyl.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:48.870
Some people prefer vinyl for this reason,
music properly mastered for the medium is

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:54.130
to a certain degree immune to the effects
of the music loudness wars and in some cases,

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:57.959
this can mean that the more nuanced parts
of the song are easier to pick out for a trained

00:11:57.959 --> 00:11:58.959
ear.

00:11:58.959 --> 00:12:03.490
Really though, given that this same information
can be recorded on a digital format and replayed

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:08.620
exactly the same, the answer to this question
is that digital and analog formats are functionally

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:13.529
the same in the quality of sound produced
and any preference for one media or the other,

00:12:13.529 --> 00:12:15.760
is really just that, a preference.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.370
The longevity and iconic status of the vinyl
record as a music format cannot be ignored

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:21.370
though.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:25.280
Despite the shortcomings we’ve described,
it is an incredibly durable and elegantly

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:26.690
simple medium.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:31.450
This is probably best exemplified in the golden
plated record sent on the Voyager 1 In September

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:32.450
of 1977.

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:37.050
It’s hard to believe but 12 billion miles
away from here this record is floating through

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:38.050
space.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:42.070
It’s cover contains simple instructions
for playback based on certain universal constants

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:47.790
and the record itself contains a high resolution
snapshot of 200,000 years of human culture.

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:52.600
Unless the Voyager 1 suffers a direct impact
or encounter heat that may melt the record,

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:56.720
this record in theory, should out survive
even our species.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:01.470
Just as the technology for storing music has
advanced the technology mixing and mastering

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:06.180
music has, it has never been easier to get
into music production, thanks to programs

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:10.470
like FL Studio and Ableton which give you
a virtual production room with all the tools

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:12.269
you need to create a song of your own.

00:13:12.269 --> 00:13:16.529
There has never been more information available
to learn how to use them either.

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:21.690
With introductory classes like this on Skillshare
for FL Studio and Ableton, and many more classes

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:26.860
to teach you the nitty gritty of music production
like this one from Grammy nominated DJ Young

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:27.860
Guru.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:31.220
These days you can teach yourself pretty much
any skill online and Skillshare is a fantastic

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:32.620
place to do it.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:36.690
With professional and understandable classes,
that follow a clear learning curve, you can

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.450
dive in and start learning how to do the work
you love.

